## R4 Decision: Stateless Visual Sync Job

Problem: The visual oscillator job still generated the mock predator signal from inside the 50k coral color job. That couples mock input production to the hot visual transform and violates the stateless-kernel rule.

Solution: Move mock predator production to a one-record vault producer before pulse consumption. Keep the Burst visual job limited to transforming vault glow/palette/pulse inputs into packed GPU colors and four global Dear Lie groups.

Rejected Alternatives: Keeping predator generation in the visual job was cheap, but it polluted the hot path with unrelated state mutation. Scheduling a second mock job would add another fence/lock path for one DTO.

Scalability potential: Low tier keeps one trivial mock write and one visual job. Middle/high/ultra tiers can replace the mock producer with real typed signals without changing the oscillator job.

Hardware Impact: Estimated CPU gain is small per frame (<1 us on desktop, visible mainly as lower lock contention on weak CPUs). Architectural gain is larger: fewer vault locks around the visual job and cleaner Burst scheduling.

## R4 Decision: Unity Job Safety Finalization

Problem: Unity NativeArray reads after a scheduled job require `JobHandle.Complete()` to release safety handles before CPU-side publication. A blind `.Complete()` inside `Tick()` would be a frame-time stall.

Solution: Retain a guarded finalization point only after `IsCompleted` has returned true, and label shutdown-only blocking completion separately. This is not a `Schedule().Complete()` pattern and does not wait for worker execution in the frame path.

Rejected Alternatives: Reading native memory without finalization would break safety and hide race conditions. Uploading stale globals forever would break the visible pulse sync.

Scalability potential: Weak devices can skip publish on job overrun and stay on previous shader globals; high-end devices publish every frame.

Hardware Impact: Guarded finalization avoids main-thread worker waits. Overrun path trades one-frame visual latency for frame stability.

## R4 Decision: Species Wave Speed Without Hot DTO Expansion

Problem: The editor facade required a species-level `WaveSpeed` control, but expanding `GlowStateDTO` would multiply the cost across 50,000 instances and damage the four-record-per-cache-line layout.

Solution: Add `WaveSpeed` to `BiolumSpeciesTuningDTO` only, align that cold tuning row to 24 bytes, and sample up to four species rows only when creating a predator-triggered `SyncPulseDTO`. The 50k oscillator still reads the 16-byte `GlowStateDTO`.

Rejected Alternatives: Adding `WaveSpeed` to every glow instance would add roughly 400 KB of hot memory pressure for a value that changes rarely. Performing a 150-row species lookup per glow in the Burst job was rejected as O(50k*species) waste.

Scalability potential: Low tier gets the same four-group Dear Lie and one pulse speed sample. Middle/high/ultra tiers can use richer per-species pulse authoring without changing the hot instance layout.

Hardware Impact: Estimated 0 us added to the 50k oscillator. Predator pulse creation samples at most four cold rows, estimated <2 us on low-end CPUs per event, not per frame.

