## R5 Decision: LateFrame Visual Sync Publication

Problem: The previous guarded `JobHandle.Complete()` was technically non-blocking after `IsCompleted`, but it still lived at the top of `Tick()`. That made the hot update lane responsible for safety-handle release, CPU readback, GPU buffer upload, and global shader publication.

Solution: Add `ILateFrameTickable` to `BiolumPulseSyncRuntime`, register it through `GlobalRegistry.TryRegisterLateFrameTickable`, and move publication to `LateFrameTick()`. `Tick()` now consumes typed inputs, updates mock/scalar state, and schedules the visual job only when the late-frame lane is registered.

Rejected Alternatives: Keeping the readback in `Tick()` was simpler but violated the dispatcher lane policy. Blindly removing `Complete()` was rejected because Unity safety handles still need owner-side release before CPU reads and GPU publish.

Scalability potential: Low and middle tiers can skip a late-frame publish when the job overruns and hold previous shader globals. High and ultra tiers publish every frame and spend the saved hot-lane time on richer shader-side bloom/SSGI response without changing gameplay truth.

Hardware Impact: The gain is avoiding a possible mid-update fence rather than a fixed arithmetic saving. On weak CPUs this protects the update lane from a one-frame worker timing spike; expected cost remains near 0 us when the job already completed.

## R5 Decision: UI Toolkit Editor Facade

Problem: The editor tuner used IMGUI `OnGUI()`/`GUILayout`, which creates layout churn and violates the current no-IMGUI polish mandate even though the allocations are editor-only.

Solution: Rebuild the facade as a UI Toolkit `CreateGUI()` window. Weather sliders, global pulse trigger, species color fields, base frequency sliders, and wave speed sliders still write through the existing DataVault bridge methods. Scene pulse origin uses the active `SceneView` camera only; no `Camera.main` scan remains.

Rejected Alternatives: Leaving IMGUI in place was editor-only but failed the explicit facade polish requirement. Adding a runtime UI was rejected because Task 18/20 requires an editor facade, not gameplay UI.

Scalability potential: Runtime cost is zero on all tiers. Designers keep hot-play tuning access without forcing C# recompiles; low/middle/high/ultra values can be authored through unmanaged vault rows.

Hardware Impact: Runtime impact is 0 us. Editor impact is lower repaint/layout churn versus IMGUI, with fixed controls built once per window creation.

## R5 Decision: Fail-Closed Layout Validation

Problem: Struct layout drift previously threw `InvalidOperationException` in `OnEnable()`. A gameplay exception is a bad failure mode for a visual system that owns vault handles and GPU buffers.

Solution: Convert validation to `bool ValidateSyncLayouts()`. On failure, log the violated DTO contract and disable the component before hot-swap, update, late-frame, vault, or GPU registration.

Rejected Alternatives: Continuing after a broken struct size would corrupt GPU/vault interpretation. Throwing preserved a clear error but violated the project exception policy.

Scalability potential: All hardware tiers fail identically before runtime mutation if a contract breaks. This preserves DataVault sovereignty and avoids partial GPU state publication.

Hardware Impact: Normal runtime impact is 0 us. Fault impact is bounded to component disable instead of repeated exception cost or corrupted visual memory.
